description.narrative:
In 2025, ZAMNEX demonstrated strong performance, high relevance, and adaptive effectiveness. The programme not only achieved planned outputs across all components but, in key areas, exceeded original expectations by leveraging emerging institutional opportunities to contribute to higherlevel outcomes in Zambian mining governance. Implementation was characterised by strategic responsiveness, strong national ownership, and effective use of technical assistance including an emergency response to the Sino mine disaster.

The following are some notable results achieved in 2025:

Component 1  Mine Closure from a Sustainability Perspective
ZAMNEX made a substantive contribution to systemic governance outcomes by advancing Zambias first national mine closure framework. Through adaptive management, the programme responded to overlapping initiatives arising from the World Bank supported ZMERIP project and repositioned itself as a strategic partner to the Ministry of Mines and Minerals Development (MMMD) and the Mine Safety Department (MSD).


Key outputlevel results achieved in 2025 include under this component included:

-Formal establishment of a multistakeholder Mine Closure Plan Guidelines (MCPG) Task Force;
-Development and approval of Terms of Reference for the Task Force, endorsed by MMMD;
-Official launch of the National MCPG development process;
-Delivery of a highlevel benchmarking workshop integrating Swedish, international, and Zambian technical expertise.

These outputs directly contribute to Outcomelevel change by strengthening institutional capacity, policy coherence, and government ownership of mine closure processes. 

Component 2  CSR Adaptation in the Mining Sector

ZAMNEX fully delivered on all planned 2025 outputs under Component 2, maintaining high effectiveness and implementation fidelity. The programme advanced the development of the National CSR Guiding Framework for Zambia (NGFZ).


Key results achieved include:

-Completion of strategic analytical inputs informing the NGFZ;
-Implementation of six structured Focus Group Workshops to ensure inclusive stakeholder input;
-Conduct of two Task Force validation workshops to assess and strengthen framework coherence;
-Continuous drafting, validation, and refinement of the NGFZ;
-Strengthened partnerships and stakeholder ownership across government, industry, and civil society.

These results contribute directly to intermediate outcomes related to improved CSR governance, shared standards, and increased readiness for responsible mining practices, positioning the framework for formal adoption and piloting in 2026.

Component 3  Early Warning System Adapted to the Mining Sector
ZAMNEX sustained progress toward an Impact-Based Early Warning System (IBWS) adapted to the mining sector. Results achieved in 2025 focused on foundational and analytical outputs, including system design, contextual analysis, and institutional mapping.


Key contributions included:
-Advancement of conceptual and technical design of the IBWS;
-Improved understanding of institutional requirements, processes, and tools relevant to miningrelated early warning;
-Preparation for transition from analytical design to practical testing and stakeholder engagement.

These results contribute to longterm risk management and resilience outcomes by establishing the technical and institutional groundwork for operationalisation in subsequent phases.
